WEBVTT

00:00:00.188 --> 00:00:03.195
CIMI learns dexterity from demonstrated contact.

00:00:03.917 --> 00:00:06.863
Human-centric methods preserve natural dexterity,

00:00:07.043 --> 00:00:09.593
but retargeting may misalign contact.

00:00:09.772 --> 00:00:12.415
Robot-centric interfaces ease transfer,

00:00:12.595 --> 00:00:16.556
but their hardware can constrain natural human dexterity during demonstration.

00:00:17.479 --> 00:00:24.467
Through a shared contact interface, CIMI transfers human-demonstrated skills across robot hands.

00:00:25.136 --> 00:00:27.870
Each CIMI demonstration combines multiple views.

00:00:28.036 --> 00:00:30.893
Contact cameras show finger-object interaction.

00:00:31.036 --> 00:00:33.796
Wrist and web-space cameras capture the wider scene.

00:00:33.936 --> 00:00:36.615
Tracking cameras recover contact-surface motion.

00:00:44.707 --> 00:00:49.754
From human videos, tracking and calibrated transforms recover contact-surface motion.

00:00:50.607 --> 00:00:55.206
Retargeted contact-surface trajectories supervise robot actions.

00:00:56.107 --> 00:01:00.904
Context and contact observations, with relative contact poses, feed the planner.

00:01:01.407 --> 00:01:06.655
Its predicted contact trajectories guide a controller that coordinates wrist and fingers.

00:01:07.377 --> 00:01:16.119
Now, we pair human demonstrations with autonomous LEAP rollouts. Matching views show the coin’s edge engagement, wedging, and lift.

00:01:17.377 --> 00:01:22.489
Matching fingertip views show how the human and LEAP pinch the one-centimeter cube.

00:01:24.606 --> 00:01:28.273
The human and LEAP follow the same longer sequence to retrieve a letter.

00:01:28.606 --> 00:01:30.482
The fingertip wedges beneath the flap,

00:01:33.106 --> 00:01:33.676
opens it,

00:01:39.106 --> 00:01:40.744
then pinches and extracts the letter.

00:01:45.527 --> 00:01:50.912
For the bulb, shared views reveal three-finger coordination through repeated screwing and releasing.

00:01:51.327 --> 00:01:55.303
Local feedback supports the changing contacts until the bulb seats and lights.

00:01:57.883 --> 00:01:59.405
Four key observations.

00:01:59.733 --> 00:02:03.309
First, different robot hands learn from the same human demonstrations.

00:02:03.533 --> 00:02:06.356
Shared contact geometry enables reuse.

00:02:06.583 --> 00:02:10.618
Across LEAP and Wuji, overall success reaches eighty point eight percent.

00:02:11.841 --> 00:02:15.658
Second, contact cameras improve contact establishment.

00:02:15.941 --> 00:02:22.621
Local views guide precise fingertip contact. Without them, the baseline misses or displaces objects.

00:02:22.991 --> 00:02:27.922
Adding contact cameras raises mean success by seventy-nine point one percentage points.

00:02:28.902 --> 00:02:33.592
Third, predicting contact-surface trajectories improves task success.

00:02:33.752 --> 00:02:37.450
Coordinating wrist and fingers helps avoid these baseline failures.

00:02:37.602 --> 00:02:41.586
Mean success across tasks improves by fifty percentage points.

00:02:42.366 --> 00:02:48.818
Freeing the wrist improves fingertip alignment. Reprojection corrects the resulting context-view mismatch.

00:02:49.666 --> 00:02:53.875
Coin and bulb success rise from zero to eighty-five and ninety percent.

00:02:55.190 --> 00:02:58.126
Supplementary materials are available on our website.
